// Module `result`: explicit error conversion for `?` (LANG-18). `e?` propagates an error
// only when the function's error type is exactly e's; anything else is converted first.
// These two cover the conversions the nine workloads repeat; a one-off is a `match`.

/// r with its error mapped by f; Ok is passed through untouched (Rust map_err). f is a
/// `scoped fn` (OWN-21): it may view the caller's locals, does not wait and does not
/// escape the call. Alloc: nothing of its own. Cost: O(1) plus f.
/// Ex: `let n = result.map_err(int.parse(field), fn(take e: ParseError) -> AppError { BadNumber(e) })?`
pub fn map_err[T, E, F](take r: Result[T, E], f: scoped fn(take E) -> F) -> Result[T, F]

/// r with the argument an allocating core operation gave back dropped from its error,
/// leaving the Capacity (`list.push`, `box.new` and `pool.insert` return what they would
/// have taken, core.md "Errors"). The argument is cleaned. Cost: O(1) plus that cleanup.
/// Ex: `result.capacity_only(list.push(mut path, v))?` in a function returning `Result[_, Capacity]`
pub fn capacity_only[T, X](take r: Result[T, (Capacity, X)]) -> Result[T, Capacity]
